                      %a{:href => "#InNegativeCodimension"} In negative codimension
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#thoms_construction"} Thom’s construction
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#lashofs_construction"} Lashof’s construction
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_divisors"} Relation to divisors
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology+theory"} Whitehead-generalized cohomology theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} represented
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+spectrum"} Thom spectrum
            is called a
            %em cobordism cohomology theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Atiyah61"}> Atiyah 61
            ), in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
            with the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+homology+theory"} generalized homology theory
            called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bordism+homology+theory"} bordism homology theory
          %p
            In both cases, a version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontryagin-Thom+construction"} Pontryagin-Thom construction
            identifies the (co)homology classes of these (co)homology theories with
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bordism"} bordism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            (carrying some given
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bordism+homology+theory"} bordism homology theory
            this was understood since the very inception of the subject (
            %a{:href => "#Thom54"}> Thom 54
            ), while for cobordism cohomology theory this identification is made explicit in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Atiyah61"} Atiyah 61, Sec. 3
            %a{:href => "#Quillen71"} Quillen 71
            (relying on results from
            %a{:href => "#Thom54"} Thom 54
            nonetheless), see below at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#GeometricModel"} Geometric model via cobordism classes
          %p
            Accordingly, cobordism cohomology theories are fundamental concepts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bordism+theory"} bordism theory
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+topology"} differential topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theories"} complex oriented cohomology theories
            (with its variants such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternionic+oriented+cohomology"} quaternionic-oriented theories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+homotopy+theory"} chromatic homotopy theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+complex+orientation+on+MU"} universal complex orientation on MU
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+topology"} differential topology
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            with deep issues in abstract
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            There are many different flavours of cobordism cohomology theories (see the list of
            %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
            below), depending on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangential+structure"} tangential structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            encoded in the representing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+spectrum"} Thom spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M f
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} represented
                by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MO"} MO
                is the base case, in the sense that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} orthogonal group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi O
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalent
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+linear+group"} general linear group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi GL
                        %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL_{\mathbb{R}}(n)
                (namely the
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+compact+subgroup"} maximal compact subgroup
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi O
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangential+structure"} tangential structure
                corresponds to no extra
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangential+structure"} tangential structure
            %li
              %p
                The cohomology theory represented by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MU"} MU
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism+cohomology"} complex cobordism cohomology
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periodic+cohomology+theory"} periodic cohomology theory
                version is sometimes denoted
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MP"} MP
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MO"} MO
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MU"} MU
                are unified by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology+theory"} equivariant cohomology theory
                called
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MR-theory"} MR-theory
            %li
              %p
                On the other hand,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+manifold"} framed
                cobordism cohomology theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MFr"} MFr
                is equivalently
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+Cohomotopy+theory"} stable Cohomotopy theory
                (by the
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontryagin-Thom+theorem"} Pontryagin-Thom theorem
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy+theory"} Cohomotopy theory
                this – and its relation to cobordism classes of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normally+framed+submanifolds"} normally framed submanifolds
                (the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "cohomotopy#RelationToCobordismGroup"} Pontryagin construction
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+theory"} cobordism theory
                (in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Pontrjagin38"} Pontrjagin 38
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Pontrjagin55"} Pontrjagin 55
          %h2#GeometricModel Geometric model via cobordism classes
          %p
            We discuss a geometric model for the cobordism cohomology theory, due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Quillen71"} Quillen 71, Section 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+spectrum"} Thom spectrum
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MU"} MU
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} M
                    %msup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mo +
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi q
                    %mi MU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{M} \mathrm{U}^q(X) \;\coloneqq\; [\Sigma^\infty X_+, \Sigma^q MU]
              is equivalently the set of
              %a{:href => "#CobordismClassesOfMaps"} cobordism classes
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+maps"} proper
              %a{:href => "#ComplexOrientedMaps"} complex-oriented
              maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon Z \to X
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Quillen71"}> Quillen 71, Prop. 1.2
            )
          %p This uses the following definitions:
          #ComplexOrientedMaps.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong (complex-oriented maps)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon Z \to X
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+map"} smooth map
            %p
              If the relative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is even at all points of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              %em complex orientation
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
              of factorizations of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              in the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi E
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    p \circ i
                    \\;\colon\;
                    Z
                    \\longrightarrow
                    E
                    \\longrightarrow
                    X
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon E\to X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+bundle"} complex vector bundle
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon Z\to E
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embedding+of+topological+spaces"} embedding
              equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+structure"} complex structure
              on its
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+bundle"} normal bundle
            %p
              Two such factorizations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i,p)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i',p')
              are regarded as equivalent if there is another factorization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo ″
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i'',p'')
              together with embeddings of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+bundles"} complex vector bundles
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to E'
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                    %mo ″
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to E''
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ″
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                    %mo ″
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i''\colon X\times[0,1]\to E''\times [0,1]
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
              equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+structure"} complex structure
              on its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+bundle"} normal bundle
              that restricts to the corresponding complex structures on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times \{0\}
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times \{1\}
          #CobordismClassesOfMaps.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (cobordism classes of maps)
            %p
              Here two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+map"} proper
              %a{:href => "#ComplexOrientedMaps"} complex-oriented
              maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i \colon Z_i \to X
              are called
              %em cobordant
              if there is a proper complex-oriented map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\colon W\to X\times\mathbf{R}
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times\{0\}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times\{1\}
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transversal+map"} transversal
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              and pulling back
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              to these submanifolds yields
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Quillen71"}> Quillen 71, p. 31
            )
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %div
            %p
              %strong>
                flavors of
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bordism+homology+theories"} bordism homology theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+cohomology+theories"} cobordism cohomology theories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} representing
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+spectra"} Thom spectra
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+rings"} cobordism rings
              \:
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bordism+homology+theory"} bordism theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M%28B%2Cf%29"} M(B,f)
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-bordism"}> B-bordism
              ):
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MFr"} MFr
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MO"}> MO
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MSO"} MSO
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MSpin"} MSpin
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MString"} MString
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MU"}> MU
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MSU"} MSU
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ravenel%27s+spectrum"} MΩΩSU(n)
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MP-theory"}> MP
                  ,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MR-theory"} MR
